      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let M(n) represent the number of bacteria after n days. The given information translates to this system of equations:\n\n$\\begin{cases}\nM(0) = 3\\\\\nM(1) = 6\\\\\nM(2) = 12\\\\\nM(n+1) = 2\\times M(n), \\text{ when } n \\geq 1\n\\end{cases}$\n\nWe are looking for the first day such that $M(n) > 100$.\n\nUpon observation, this resembles the formal definition of $\\log_2$ (base 2 logarithm):\n\nM(n) = 1 * 2^1 + 2 * 2^2 + 4 * 2^3 + ... + 2^n\n\nWe will use the formula of the sigma function to find the smallest integer n for which $n = 2^x$\n\nΣ(#1+p{i,i})< 6328\n\nn = 5\n\n5 = 32\nP = 5\n#1 = 4\nThe written answer is 2589.\n\nThe problem requires to find the first time $M(n)$ is greater than 100. From the recurrence relation $M(n)=2\\times M(n-1))$ we get $M(n)+M(n-1)=2M(n-1) \\implies M(n)=2M(n-1)$, a geometric progression. Because $M(0)=3$, the formula becomes $M(n)=3\\cdot 2^n$ and we are looking for the smallest $n$ such that $3\\cdot 2^n>100$. It's then just a squareroot(progression), so $2n\\gt 4\\implies n\\gt 2$, and thus the solution is $2589$.",
